<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chōshū Domain</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Choshu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Choshu was a feudal domain in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, ruled by the Mori family. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, it led the opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> in the 1860s. Choshu forces were central to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>, and its leaders dominated the early government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/choshu-domain.jpg" alt="Chōshū Domain" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: by Reggaeman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagi_Castle_The_Keep_Base.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chōshū Domain, also called the Hagi Domain, was a han of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> from 1600 to 1871. It was based at Hagi Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagato-province">Nagato Province</a> and ruled throughout by the tozama daimyō of the Mōri family. Its rulers descended from the warlord Mōri Motonari, who had extended his power over all of Chūgoku and held territory worth 1,200,000 koku.</p><p>Motonari's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and, after Hideyoshi's death, was chosen titular head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a>. His cousin Kikkawa Hiroie secretly made a deal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> that left 15,000 Mōri soldiers inactive. After assurances from Ieyasu, Terumoto gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> without a fight. The Mōri were moved from Aki to Nagato, and their holdings were cut to 369,000 koku.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The reduction left a large shortfall for military upkeep, though the domain remained the seventh largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s own lands. To restore its finances the Mōri cut all retainers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, paid some in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> instead of land, and encouraged some to take up farming. A land survey found hidden mountain farms and taxed them, and the domain controlled the trade in paper, rice, salt and wax. These measures strengthened the daimyō's control but angered peasants and displaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, and revolts were frequent.</p><p>The Mōri created four subordinate domains, Iwakuni, Chōfū, Tokuyama and Kiyosue, ruled by branches of the family. The main line died out in 1707, and heirs were then adopted from the Chōfū branch, which itself died out in 1751, after which the family continued through the Kiyosue branch. The daimyō governed with the help of karō, domain elders, some of whom held the rank by heredity and some, such as the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murata-seifu">Murata Seifū</a>, for life as men of talent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Chōshū later became a centre of anti-Tokugawa activity. According to tradition, each New Year the elders asked the daimyō whether the time had come to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, and he replied that it had not, since the shogunate was still too strong. About 260 years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> the domain joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and sympathetic court nobles in the Satchō Alliance. In 1865 it bought the warship Union in Satsuma's name. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> its forces fought the armies of the former shōgun, and its forces of 1867 to 1869 formed the foundation of the Imperial Japanese Army. The domain was dissolved in 1871 and absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-prefecture">Yamaguchi Prefecture</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chōshū Domain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The southwestern tozama domains of Satsuma and <b>Choshu</b>, long resentful of Tokugawa dominance, responded by acquiring Western rifles, warships and military advisers, while the shogunate modernised with less urgency.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Forces from Satsuma and <b>Choshu</b> seized the imperial palace in Kyoto and an imperial decree abolished the Tokugawa shogunate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-3">3 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1862 Matsudaira Katamori became Kyoto protector, policing the capital with the Shinsengumi and fighting <b>Choshu</b> radicals.</blockquote><a href="/state/aizu-domain">Aizu Domain</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Choshu</b> developed paper, salt and wax and reformed its finances under Murata Seifu in the 1840s.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Mori were reduced to <b>Choshu</b> after Sekigahara, Tokugawa Ieyasu gave Aki and Bingo to Fukushima Masanori in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/state/hiroshima-domain">Hiroshima Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>In the second shogunate expedition against <b>Choshu</b> in 1866, Choshu forces under Takasugi Shinsaku crossed the straits; Kokura burned its castle and withdrew inland to Kawara.</blockquote><a href="/state/kokura-domain">Kokura Domain</a></li></ul></section>
